L000229	\subsection{Derivation of Minimal Model}\label{app:minimal_model}
L000230	
L000231	We motivate the minimal model as an approximation to the dynamics of the active lattice gas.
L000232	
L000233	
L000234	Starting with the dynamics of the density field in the full model and integrating \eqref{eq:ABP_evolution} over the particles' orientations $\theta$ yields
L000235	\begin{equation}\label{eq:dynamics_mobility}
L000236	    \partial_t \rho({x}) = - \partial_x [{J}^{(\rho)}({x})] \equiv- \partial_x \qty[ \mathcal{M}(\rho({x})) \mathcal{F}({x}) ]
L000237	\end{equation}
L000238	where $\mathcal{M}(\rho) = 1-\rho \geq 0$ plays the role of a mobility. The generalised force $\mathcal{F}({x})$ can be split into two contributions:
L000239	\begin{equation}\label{eq:gen_forces}
L000240	    \mathcal{F} = \overbrace{\partial_x \ln(1 - \rho) + \text{Pe} \, p_x}^{\mathcal{F}_\text{PS}} - \overbrace{\rho \, \partial_x V}^{\mathcal{F}_\text{barr}}
L000241	\end{equation}
L000242	where the phase-separating force $\mathcal{F}_\text{PS}$ gives rise to MIPS, and $\mathcal{F}_\text{barr}$ arises from the barrier. To simplify this expression, we first seek a closed-form expression for the polarisation $p_x$.
L000243	We first use the definition of $\vb{p}$ to write
L000244	\begin{multline}
L000245	    \partial_t p_i = \div \qty[ d_s(\rho) \grad p_i + \mathcal{D}(\rho) p_i \grad \rho + (1-\rho) p_i \grad V ] \\
L000246	    - \text{Pe}\ \div \qty[ (\mathcal{D}(\rho)-1) p_i \vb{p}] -\text{Pe}\ \sum_j \partial_j \qty[\frac{d_s(\rho)}{2} Q_{ij} ]\\
L000247	    -\text{Pe}\ \partial_i \qty[\frac{d_s(\rho)}{2} \rho] - p_i,
L000248	\end{multline}
L000249	where the nematic alignment tensor is $Q_{ij} (\vb x, t) = 2 \int f(\vb x, \theta, t) (\vb{e}_\theta)_i (\vb{e}_\theta)_j \dd \theta - \rho(\vb x,t) \delta_{ij}$.
L000250	
L000251	Focusing on regions away from the barrier, we seek an effective theory for the density alone.  To this end, we make three approximations: (i) the polarisation $\vb{p}$ relaxes faster than the density $\rho$ allowing an adiabatic elimination; (ii) density gradients are not too large, which enables gradient expansion; (iii) particles' alignment is weak, which means $|\bm{p}|^2 \ll 1$ and $\underline{\underline{Q}}\approx 0$.  This last approximation is natural, given the previous two.
L000252	
L000253	Next, considering regions away from the barrier we use weak alignment, and adiabatic elimination of the ``fast'' field $\vb{p}$, to obtain:
L000254	\begin{equation}\label{eq:p_implicit}
L000255	    p_i = \div \qty[ d_s(\rho) \grad p_i + \mathcal{D}(\rho)p_i \grad \rho] - \frac{\text{Pe}}{2} \partial_i [\rho \, d_s(\rho)]
L000256	\end{equation}
L000257	
L000258	Plugging (\ref{eq:p_implicit}) into itself recursively, and retaining only terms up to third-order in the gradient, yields
L000259	
L000260	\begin{multline}\label{eq:p_explicit_app}
L000261	    p_i = - \frac{\text{Pe}}{2} \div \bigg[ d_s(\rho) \partial_i \grad (\rho \, d_s(\rho)) \\ + \mathcal{D}(\rho) \partial_i (\rho \, d_s(\rho))\grad \rho \bigg] - \frac{\text{Pe}}{2} \partial_i [\rho \, d_s(\rho)]
L000262	\end{multline}
L000263	Physically, the main effect of the third-order gradient terms on the dynamics is to prevent density gradients from growing too large.  The same effect can be accomplished in a simpler way by approximating the polarisation as
L000264	\begin{equation}
L000265	    p_x = -\frac{\text{Pe}}{2} \partial_x [\rho \, d_s(\rho)]  + \kappa_0 \, \partial_x^3 \rho
L000266	\end{equation}
L000267	with constant $\kappa_0>0$. Substituting this expression into the phase-separating force $\mathcal{F}_\text{PS}$ from \eqref{eq:gen_forces}, and defining $\kappa = \text{Pe} \, \kappa_0$ yields:
L000268	\begin{equation}
L000269	    \mathcal{F}_\text{PS} = \partial_x \qty[ \ln(1-\rho) - \frac{\text{Pe}^2}{2} \rho \, d_s(\rho)] + \kappa\,  \partial_x^3 \rho
L000270	\end{equation}
L000271	We recognise $\mathcal{F}_\text{PS}$ as the gradient of a free energy $F_\text{PS}$:
L000272	\begin{align}
L000273	    \mathcal{F}_\text{PS} &=- \partial_x \qty(\frac{\delta F_\text{PS}}{\delta \rho}) \\
L000274	    F_\text{PS} &= \int_0^{\ell_s} \dd{x} \qty{ \frac{\kappa}{2} (\partial_x \rho)^2 + f_\rho(\rho) }
L000275	\end{align}
L000276	where the bulk free energy density is
L000277	\begin{equation}
L000278	    f_\rho(\rho) = \int \frac{\text{Pe}^2}{2} \rho \, d_s(\rho) - \ln(1-\rho) \dd{\rho}
L000279	\end{equation}
L000280	This function becomes a double-welled potential for $\text{Pe} > \text{Pe}^*$, giving rise to MIPS.
L000281	
L000282	We now seek a scalar field in one dimension $\phi(x)$ whose dynamics minimally recreates the behaviour of $\rho$, with the same periodic boundary conditions. We simplify the full dynamics by replacing $f_\rho$ with a simple double-well potential $f_\phi = \frac{\alpha}{4} \phi^2 (1-\phi)^2$. The resulting free energy of the minimal model is then \eqref{eq:minimal_F_PS}.
L000283	
L000284	To model the barrier within the minimal model, we only include its ratchet effect.  To this end,
L000285	we replace the potential $V$ with a simplified form $V(x) = \eta \, \delta(x)$ in $\mathcal{F}_\text{barr}$, yielding
L000286	\begin{align}
L000287	    \mathcal{F}_\text{barr}(\vb{x}) &= -\eta \, \rho(\vb{x}) \grad \delta(x) \\
L000288	    &=  -\eta \grad \qty[\rho(\vb{x}) \, \delta(x)] + \eta \, \delta(x) \grad \rho \,  . \label{eq:F_barr_two_terms}
L000289	\end{align}
L000290	The first term of \eqref{eq:F_barr_two_terms} models
L000291	the effect of volume exclusion from the barrier. This does not contribute to the steady-state current overall as it takes the form of a total derivative, and so its integral over space vanishes. We therefore drop this term and retain only the second term of \eqref{eq:F_barr_two_terms} in the minimal model, identifying this as a pumping (ratchet) effect of the barrier. The resulting force in the minimal model is
L000292	\begin{equation}
L000293	    \mathcal{F}^\phi_\text{pump}(x) = \eta \, (\partial_x \phi) \, \delta(x).
L000294	\end{equation}
L000295	To complete the minimal model, we replace the density-dependent mobility $\mathcal{M}(\rho)$ of \eqref{eq:dynamics_mobility} with a constant (taken without loss of generality to be $1$).
L000296	We finally arrive at the minimal model \eqref{eq:minimal_model_dynamics}. This model replaces MIPS with Cahn-Hilliard phase-separation, while the effect of the barrier is reduced to a local pumping effect at the origin; volume exclusion and all other consequences of the barrier have been removed.
L000297	
L000298	We also note that the momentum injection term in the minimal model results in a steady-state current with the same scaling as the full dynamics, in the limit of a large system.
