L000300	\subsection{Onset of Instability in the Minimal Model}
L000301	As noted above, the fully-wet state always exists as a steady-state solution of the minimal dynamics.  However, this state may not be linearly stable.   The critical wetting transition corresponds to the onset of linear instability of this state, just as in equilibrium \cite{Cahn1977, Grodzinski2026a}.
L000302	
L000303	We find this instability by considering a small perturbation of a homogeneous liquid state.   We substitute a periodic, mass-conserving perturbation
L000304	$
L000305	    \phi(x, t) = \phi_l + \sum_{n \geq 1} A_n \sin(q_n x) + B_n \cos(q_n x)
L000306	$
L000307	into the dynamics, where $q_n=2\pi n/L$; the even cosine modes play no role, and we obtain at leading order $  \dot{A}_n = \Gamma_{nm} A_m$, with
L000308	\begin{align}
L000309	   \Gamma_{nm} = - \left( \frac{\alpha}{2} q_n^2 + \kappa q_n^4 \right) \delta_{nm} + \frac{2\eta}{L} q_n  q_m
L000310	\end{align}
L000311	
L000312	The (infinite) matrix $\Gamma$ has the form
L000313	$
L000314	    \Gamma = D + \beta \vb{q} \vb{q}^T
L000315	$,
L000316	where $D = \text{diag}(\{ -\frac{\alpha}{2} q_n^2 - \kappa q_n^4 \})$ and $\beta = \frac{2\eta}{L}$. All the eigenvalues of $D$ are negative (as $\alpha, \kappa \geq 0$).
L000317	The Sherman-Morrison formula \cite{Sherman1950} states, for a rank-1 update of a diagonal matrix:
L000318	\begin{equation}
L000319	    \det(D + \beta \vb{q} \vb{q}^T - \lambda I) = \det(D- \lambda I)( 1 + \beta \vb{q}^T (D- \lambda I)^{-1} \vb{q} ).
L000320	\end{equation}
L000321	The eigenvalues of $\Gamma$ can therefore be obtained by finding the roots of its characteristic polynomial, $\det(\Gamma-\lambda I)$.  Using that $D$ is diagonal we obtain:
L000322	\begin{align}
L000323	    0 & = \det(D- \lambda I) \left( 1 + \beta \sum_{m} \frac{q_m q_m}{d_m - \lambda} \right)
L000324	\nonumber\\
L000325	    & = \prod_n \left( d_n - \lambda \right) \,  \left( 1 - \beta \sum_{m} \frac{q_m^2}{\frac{\alpha}{2} q_m^2 + \kappa q_m^4 + \lambda} \right)
L000326	\end{align}
L000327	where $d_m = -\frac{\alpha}{2} q_m^2 - \kappa q_m^4$ are the eigenvalues of $D$.
L000328	
L000329	The matrix $\Gamma$ is real-symmetric so its eigenvalues are real.  Hence the boundary of linear stability occurs when this matrix has a single zero eigenvalue, with all others being negative.  Setting $\lambda=0$ and noting that $d_n<0$ for all $n$ we find
L000330	$
L000331	0 = 1- \beta \sum_{m} \frac{1}{ (\alpha/2) + \kappa q_m^2}
L000332	$. Using the identity $\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{n^2 + a^2} = \frac{a \pi \coth(a \pi) - 1}{2a^2}$ (where $a = \frac{L}{2 \pi}\sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{2 \kappa}}$) and rearranging,  we obtain an explicit expression for the critical pumping strength $\eta^*$ at which the system becomes unstable:
L000333	\begin{equation}
L000334	    \eta^* = \frac{\sqrt{2 \alpha \kappa}}{\coth(L \sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{8 \kappa}}) - \frac{1}{L}\sqrt{\frac{8 \kappa}{\alpha}}}
L000335	\end{equation}
L000336	For large $L$, this tends to $\sqrt{2\alpha\kappa}$. Simulations confirm this instability boundary represents the onset of partial wetting, even when the ratchet is embedded in a liquid layer of finite width $L$ surrounded by vapour (instead of a periodic domain of the liquid density).
